:: Day 2 EV

Morning came up thin and yellow over the reach. Wren had slept badly on the jetty boards with Pip curled against her back, and she woke with the iron key printing its shape into her palm.

The Weir House had one door, oak, banded in iron, and it faced the water. The weir roared beside it. Pip stood well back.

"I only opened it," he said. "I didn't go in. There's a room with a table, and on the table there's a book, and the book has names in it. Our names. Yours and mine and Tam's, and a date next to each one, and the dates aren't past."

Wren put the key in the lock. It turned as if it had been oiled yesterday.

Inside, the room was exactly as Pip had said, except for the smell, which was of a lamp lately blown out. The book on the table was bound in green cloth. The wardens' ledger, or one like it.

Behind her, out on the water, someone began to pole a boat toward the weir, slow and careful, and a voice she knew called her name.

[[Open the ledger->The warden's ledger]]
[[Go out to the weir->The weir]]

:: Hollin Reach

Pip was waiting on the jetty, which was the first surprise, and he was dry, which was the second. Wren's brother was twelve and had never in his life been where he was supposed to be.

"You took your time," he said.

"Tam sent me. You went across with the fish carts."

"I went across with the fish carts," Pip agreed, "and then I went up to the old mill, because Brother Ilex gave me the key, and I'm not going to tell you what I saw there until you've seen it yourself." He was shivering under his bravado. Wren gave him her coat, the oilskin one with the torn pocket that Tam had cut down for her two winters ago, and he took it without a word, which was how she knew it was bad. Then he pulled a key from inside his shirt and gave it to her: iron, as long as her hand, and warm from his skin.

The old mill stood at the head of the reach where the weir cut the river in half. Nobody had ground flour there since before Wren was born. The wardens called it the Weir House and kept the keys, or thought they did.

"Show me," Wren said.

"Tomorrow," Pip said. "The wardens' boat goes down to the Reach fair at first light and doesn't come back till noon. We wait."

[[Day 2 EV]]

:: The widow's door

The Widow Kestle lived in the last house before the hill gave up and became cliff. Her door was painted the blue of a jay's wing and had been for as long as anyone could remember. Wren knocked, and the door opened before her knuckles left it.

"You're Tam's girl," the widow said. She was tall and straight-backed, with white hair cropped short as a boy's, and she was holding a knife and a half-peeled apple. "Come in or don't, but shut the door."

Wren came in. The room was full of clocks, none of them running.

"Your brother came by at dusk. Eleven years old and out after the bell with a key in his fist." The widow shook her head. "I told him what I'll tell you. I have lived in this house all my life, and I was never anyone's wife, whatever they call me down the hill. And I have never once set foot in the Weir House, because I know what's in it."

"What's in it?"

"Ask the man who built the weir." She went back to her apple. "He's been dead sixty years. Ask him anyway."

The widow looked at Wren for a long moment, and she did not look away.

Wren stood there a while longer, and then she went down to the ferry and crossed to Hollin Reach in the last of the light, because she could think of nothing else to do. She found Pip asleep under an upturned boat on the far jetty, with the key still in his fist.

[[Day 2 EV]]

:: Back at the ferry

Tam was mending a net on the jetty when Wren came down the hill, and did not look up until Wren had told her all of it: the key, the Weir House, the widow.

"Thirty years I've poled this river," Tamsin Reave said, "and I've never once had a warden set foot on my ferry, and I don't mean to start because a boy's gone wandering." She bit through the twine. "But he's your brother. So."

"So?"

"So we go across after dark, when the wardens are drunk at the Reach, and we don't take the lantern, because a light on the water is a thing they can see." She said it flat, the way she said everything, and Wren understood that Old Tam had decided this before Wren ever climbed the hill.

They ate the rest of Maud's stew in silence. The bell rang dusk from the hill. Tam took the long ash pole down from the rafters, the one she never let anyone else touch, and leaned it by the door.

"It's older than you are," she said. "Don't break it."

[[The dark crossing]]
